\subsubsection{Origin time prediction for phase association} 

PhaseNet+ incorporates origin time prediction as a novel task to facilitate phase association, so we need to validate its prediction accuracy to ensure reliable constraints for phase association. 
Although precise origin time needs to be determined through geophysical inversion during earthquake location, approximate origin time predictions are sufficient for phase association purposes. 
\Cref{fig:event_time_error} shows the origin time error distributions and the correlation with phase travel times. 
For both Northern and Southern California, prediction errors remain relatively stable within 2s, with a modest increase at longer distances (e.g., travel times exceeding 15s). This is expected due to stronger impacts of velocity model inaccuracies and limited large events in model training. 
The origin time errors are smaller than typical earthquake intervals of enhanced earthquake catalogs, such as the QTM catalog, which has an average inter-event time of 174s and merged detections separated by less than 2s during template matching \citep{ross2019searching}. Therefore, the origin time prediction task could be effectively applied to constrain the identification of coherent phase arrivals from the same seismic event, thereby streamlining the phase association process.
Additionally, the comparable performance between Northern and Southern California datasets underscores the applicability of PhaseNet+'s origin time predictions across various seismic network configurations and geological settings.

