{\it General idea for SMT and SHE}. Let us first explain the general idea of SMT in non-Hermitian systems. This can be understood using a rather simple model, $H = p^2/2 + i\alpha p$, where $\alpha$ is the strength of dissipation ($\alpha>0$) or gain ($\alpha<0$), and the wavefunction follows open boundary conditions $\psi(0) = \psi(L) = 0$. We have eigenstates of $H$ as $\psi_m^R(x)=c_R \mathrm{ sin}\frac{m\pi x}{L} e^{\alpha x}$ and $H^\dagger$ as $
\psi_m^L(x)=c_L \mathrm{ sin}\frac{m\pi x}{L} e^{-\alpha x}$, where $m$ labels the states and $c_L^\ast c_R=\frac{2}{L} $ are chosen to satisfy the biorthogonal condition $\int_0^L \psi_m^{L\ast}(x) \psi_n^R(x) dx = \delta_{mn}$. Then suppose a perturbation as $\delta V = \delta v \delta(x-x_0)$, we have SMT defined as
\begin{equation}
    \chi_{m}={\langle \psi_m^L |\delta V |\psi_m^R \rangle \over \delta v} =\frac{2}{L} \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{m \pi}{L} x_{0}\right),
    \label{eq-chidef}
\end{equation}
% following the definition of susceptibility in statistical physics.
which is a measure of the change of the eigenenergy due to the change in the potential at the lattice end. Notably, $\chi_m$ is independent of $\alpha$, showing that skin modes in an open system, despite their vanishing amplitude at one boundary, retain energy sensitivity to edge perturbations.

This tunability arises from a fundamental argument. Consider a sufficiently large N-site chain where skin modes are strongly localized on the left, with negligible amplitude at the right end. If we remove the rightmost site, conventional perturbation theory would suggest the wave functions remain largely unchanged. However, this would imply $N$ skin modes with $N-1$ lattice sites, a contradiction. Thus, even if the amplitude of the wave function is negligible at the boundary, these states must be sensitive to local potentials. The failure of perturbation theory comes from the non-orthogonality of eigenstates in non-Hermitian Hamiltonians, requiring separate left and right eigenstates \cite{brody2013biorthogonal, Kunst2018Biorthogonal}. These features make the SMT an intrinsic property of such systems. 

%can be understood from the following basic argument. For a chain with $N$ sites, let us assume that all skin modes are well localized on the left part, with almost vanished amplitude at the right end, which can always be realized when $N$ is sufficient long. Let us imagining to remove the rightmost site, then if the conventional perturbation theory holds, then the wave functions will not be significantly affected. This will lead to $N$ skin modes, yet with only $(N-1)$ lattice site, leading to contradiction. Thus, with this argument, we can say that no matter how small the wave functions are occupied at the rightmost end, it can be tuned by the local potential at the right end due to the failure of perturbation theory, which applies to all non-Hermitian models not necessary at to be around the symmetry breaking point. This contradiction also explains why in the non-Hermitian models the left and right eigenstates should be determined by $H$ and $H^\dagger$, respectively, hence the SMT defined above should be an intrinsic feature of the non-Hermitian models. 

As a direct manifestation, we will explore its possible application in SHE. Here, SHE refers to the ability of a wave to reconstruct itself after encountering an obstacle, maintaining its overall structure and propagation characteristics \cite{longhi2022self,bouchal1998self,mcgloin2005bessel,broky2008self,kim2018multiple,antonacci2019demonstration,thachil2024self}. This is also a fundamental phenomenon of non-Hermitian models since the similar phenomena is impossible to happen in Hermitian models. Consider a Hamiltonian $H$ with right eigenstate $|\psi_i^{R}\rangle$ corresponding to eigenvalue  $\epsilon_i$ and left eigenstate $|\psi_i^{L}\rangle$. We focus on the time evolution of the eigenstate $|\psi_{i_s}^R\rangle$ with largest gain where $z$ donates time. Under a perturbation that end at $z_1$, the wave function is  $|\Psi^{\prime}(z_1)\rangle=\sum_{i} c_{i} |\psi_{i}^R\rangle$, where $c_{i}=\langle\psi_{i}^{L} \mid \Psi^{\prime}(z_1)\rangle$. The subsequent dynamics, governed by 
\begin{equation}
    |\Psi^\prime(z)\rangle= e^{-i H (z-z_1)}|\Psi^{\prime}(z_1)\rangle=\sum_{i} c_{i} e^{ -i\epsilon_{i} (z-z_1)} |\psi_{i}^R\rangle,
\end{equation}
which is dominated at long distances by $|\psi_{i_s}\rangle$, thus the initial beam is reconstructed. This idea is essentially identical to the imaginary evolution process in searching of ground state, yet here it search the skin mode with largest gain \cite{hoffmann1996computational}. In experiment, this ability requires that this state should be isolated from all the continuous spectra in a infinite system. In another words, only one state with the largest gain can be self-healed and the SHE requires the ability to tune eigenvalues,

