{\it Floquet lattice, non-Hermitian Hamiltonian and winding number}. We provide a concrete physical platform based on a photonic Floquet lattice to realize the self-healed state (SHS) using the technique of SMT. By engineering the right end potential, the energy of the most susceptible state can be modulated, and the SHE can be observed when the SHS is well separated from all the other spectra. We demonstrate this feasibility in photonic Floquet lattice and estimate the relevant physical parameters using the current experimental setup. 

The physical system is presented in Fig. \ref{fig2}(a). In this model, each unit cell contains two waveguides with opposite loss and gain, $\pm \gamma$. The propagation described by Maxwell's equations can correspond to the description by the Schrödinger-type equation \cite{chen2021tight}.  Let us write the $z$-dependent Hamiltonian taking periodic boundary conditions as $\mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z)$, which is a $N  \times N$ matrix:
\begin{eqnarray}
    &&  \mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z) = \sum_n -i\gamma c_{2n-1}^\dagger c_{2n-1} + i\gamma c_{2n}^\dagger c_{2n} +  \\
       && \kappa e^{-i\mathbf{A}(z)\cdot \mathbf{r}_1} c_{2n-1}^\dagger c_{2n} 
       + \kappa e^{-i\mathbf{A}(z)\cdot \mathbf{r}_2} c_{2n}^\dagger c_{2n+1} + \text{h.c.}, \nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}
which takes periodic connections, where $c_n^\dagger$ $(c_n)$ is the creation (annihilation) operator at site $n$, $z$ plays the role of time studied in the Schrodinger equation. $\kappa$ is the original nearest-neighbor coupling coefficient dependent on the distance of waveguide $d$, and $\mathbf{r}_{1,2} = (d\cos\theta,\pm d\sin\theta)$ are the two vectors that describe the zigzag structure, with $\theta$ being the zigzag angle. The Floquet modulation 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}(z)=k_{0} R \Omega(\sin (\Omega z), -\cos (\Omega z), 0),
\end{equation}
is the gauge potential and its interplay with gain and loss results in the skin effect \cite{yi2020non,li2022gain,lin2021steering,wu2022flux,li2023loss,sun2024photonic}. $k_0=2 \pi n_{0} / \lambda$, $n_0$ is the refractive index of the substrate and $\lambda$ is the wavelength. The helical radius is $R$, the helical period is $Z$, and $\Omega=2\pi/Z$.  With the typical values in experiments, we find $\mathcal{O}(k_0 R \Omega d) \sim 1$, thus we can tune the effective coupling between the neighboring sites using this Floquet modulation. 

