\includegraphics[width=0.46\textwidth]{fig2.pdf}
\caption{\label{fig-epsart}(a) Floquet lattice for SHE using a zigzag helical waveguide array. (b) The schematic representation of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$, which is the effective Hamiltonian of $\mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z)$. (c) The schematic representation of $
\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$ which is the effective Hamiltonian of $\mathcal{H}^\text{OBC}(z)$. (d) The schematic representation of $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$. After modulating the potential with strength $\delta V$ at the right end of $\mathcal{H}^\text{OBC}(z)$, it will gain an equivalent term in its effective Hamiltonian. Long-range interactions beyond next-nearest neighbors not shown for clarity in (b)(c)(d).}
\label{fig2}
\end{figure}

The effective Hamiltonian of a Floquet system with periodic time driving \cite{rechtsman2013photonic,maczewsky2017observation,yang2020photonic,biesenthal2022fractal,li2023loss,lin2024observation,sun2024photonic,lindner2011floquet,aidelsburger2013realization,atala2014observation,jotzu2014experimental,dalibard2011colloquium,aidelsburger2011experimental,goldman2014periodically} is obtained through the time evolution operator in a full period $Z$. Numerically, we can obtain the effective Hamiltonian of $\mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z)$ donated as $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ using the following formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}} = {i \over Z} \ln \prod_{i=1}^{K} \exp(-i\mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z_i) \delta z), \quad \delta z = {Z \over K},
\label{eq-Heffdis}
\end{equation}
with $K$ to be large enough (we choose $K = 1000$). In this way, in principle, $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ is a dense non-Hermitian matrix. From the numerical calculation, the matrix elements $(\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}})_{ij}$ are negligible for the physics when $|i-j| > 3$.  By keeping only the relevant terms (Fig. \ref{fig2}(b)), $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ is described by 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}=& \sum_{n }  \mathbf{\psi}_{n}^{\dagger} \epsilon_{n} \mathbf{\psi}_{n}+ \sum_{n} \sum_{k = \pm 1, \pm 2}
      \mathbf{\psi}_{n}^{\dagger} \mathbf{J}_{-\mathbf{k}} \mathbf{\psi}_{n+\mathbf{k}}
\end{split},
\label{eq-Hinfinity}
\end{equation}
which takes periodic connections, where $\mathbf{\psi}_{n}= \left(\begin{array}{cc}
a_{n} \\
b_{n}
\end{array}\right)$, $\mathbf{\epsilon}_{n}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
M & t_{-1} \\
t_1 & -M
\end{array}\right)$, $\mathbf{J_{-1}}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
t_{-2} & t_{-3} \\
t_{-1}^\ast & t_{-2}^\ast
\end{array}\right)$, $\mathbf{J_{1}}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
t_{2} & t_{1}^\ast \\
t_3 & t_{2}^\ast
\end{array}\right)$, $\mathbf{J_{-2}}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 0 \\
t_{-3}^\ast & 0
\end{array}\right)$ and $\mathbf{J_{2}}=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0 & t_3^\ast \\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right)$. Here $t_{\pm j}$ is the hopping between sites with distance $j$, with $\pm$ denotes the direction, and $\pm M$ is the effective onsite potential in each unit cell.

The specific implementation parameters are determined as follows. We choose the wavelength $\lambda=633 ~\mathrm{nm}^{-1}$ and $n_0=1.505$. The coupling coefficient is $\kappa=0.25 ~\mathrm{mm}^{-1}$ when $d=11 ~\mu \mathrm{m}$ \cite{wang2022edge}. We choose period $Z=5 ~\mathrm{mm}$ to avoid losses caused by waveguide bending and the setups are close to those reported in previously realized helical waveguides \cite{rechtsman2013photonic,sun2024photonic}. The zigzag angle is $\pi/6$ to ensure only nearest-neighbor coupling exist in $\mathcal{H}^\text{PBC}(z)$. The loss and gain amplitude is $\gamma=0.14 \kappa$, which can be experimentally realized with only loss by introducing a global decay factor \cite{xia2021nonlinear}.
% Besides, a sufficiently large $\gamma$ is chosen to ensure that, even after applying a certain amplitude of additional right-end gain, the tunable state still exhibits a leftward skin effect.

The numerical results of the effective couplings $t_{\pm j}$ are shown in Fig. \ref{fig3}, which is a function of $R$. For the static case ($R=0$), where the system reduces to a conventional waveguide array with $t_{\pm 1}=\kappa$, while higher-order couplings are negligible due to their exponential decay with separation. As $R$ increases, the higher-order effective couplings $t_{\pm2}$ emerge, and the first- and second-order couplings in the two directions differ slightly \cite{sun2024photonic,huang2021loss,li2023loss}. Notably, the non-zero phase of $t_{\pm2}$ induces an effective gauge field, remaining fixed at $\pm \pi/2$ over a wide range of $R$, which is the primary origin of the non-Hermitian skin effect in the Floquet lattices. In our case ($R=10.45 ~\mu \mathrm{m}$) where the first- and second-order coupling strengths are comparable, we choose the coupling terms up to the third order to accurately describe the system.
