\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{fig3.pdf}% Here is how to import EPS art.
\caption{The amplitude (a) and phase (b) of the coefficient $t_{\pm 1,\pm 2}$ as a function of $R$ from the Floquet modulation. We choose $10.45 ~\mu \mathrm{m}$ }
\label{fig3}. 
\end{figure}

To understand the physics in this work, we consider the Hamiltonian Eq. (\ref{eq-Hinfinity}) in the momentum space
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
& \mathcal{H}_\text{F}(k) =\\
& \begin{pmatrix}
M + t_{2} /\beta + t_{-2}\beta & 
 t_{-1} + t_{1}^\ast/ \beta + t_{-3}\beta + t_{3}^\ast/\beta^2\\[1em]
 t_{1} + t_{-1}^\ast\beta + t_{3} /\beta + t_{-3}^\ast\beta^2& 
-M + t_{2}^\ast/\beta + t_{-2}^\ast\beta
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
It is a two-band model, with $E_\pm(k)$ being its two eigenvalues. The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$, which is composed of $E_\pm(k)$ where $k$ varies on Brillouin zone (BZ) (real values of $[-\pi, \pi]$), form a closed loop in a complex energy plane as shown in Fig. \ref{fig4}(a). The calculated the generalized Brillouin zone (GBZ), which is constituted by two sub-GBZs, $\text{GBZ}_+$ and $\text{GBZ}_-$ \cite{yang2020non}, is inside the unit circle as shown in Fig. \ref{fig4}(b) indicating the existence of skin effect. $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ is a matrix derived from $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ by removing the periodic connections at the top-right and bottom-left corners. The bulk-band spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ in the thermodynamic limit is composed of $E_\pm(\beta)$ (substitute $e^{ik}$ with $\beta$) where $\beta$ varies on $\text{GBZ}_\pm$, even in the presence of perturbations near the edges \cite{yao2018edge}. The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ is rather distinct from that of  $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ and and lies entirely within the spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ as presented in Fig. \ref{fig4}(a). The point-gap topology of spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ is characterized by the winding number $W(E)$ derived from $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}(k)$, which is defined as
\begin{equation}
    W(E)=\frac{1}{2 \pi i} \oint_{\mathrm{BZ}} \partial_{k} \ln \operatorname{det}\left[\mathcal{H}_\text{F}(k)-E\right] d k.
\end{equation}
The winding of the spectrum is $W=1$ indicating the eigenstate of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ localizing at the left boundary \cite{okuma2020topological,zhang2020correspondence}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{fig4.pdf}
\caption{(a) The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$ forms a loop and exhibit a nonzero winding number $W=1$. The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ in the thermodynamic limit is inside the spectrum of 
$\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{PBC}}$. (b) The $\text{GBZ}$ is entirely inside the unit circle, also indicating that all eigenstates of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ are skin modes localize on the left side. The two sub-GBZs nearly coincide under our parameters. (c) The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ with the number of unit cells $N=50$. The state $|\psi_{i_s}\rangle$ with the maximum SMT is marked with a red circle. (d) The spectrum of effective Hamiltonian $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ when a controlled potential $\delta V c_{2N}^\dagger c_{2N} $ with $\delta V=0.06i\kappa$ is applied at right end of $\mathcal{H}^\text{OBC}(z)$. Compared to $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$, the energy of most skin modes remains nearly unchanged, except $|\psi_{i_s}\rangle$. (e) Spatial distribution of each eigenstates  $|\psi_i\rangle$ with $|\psi_{i_s}\rangle$ marked in red line. (f) $|\chi_i|$  of each state,  incicating $i_s=50$ is the skin mode with the maximum SMT being the SHS.}\label{fig4}
\end{figure}

{\it Boundary manipulation and SHE}. With the above result, we then move to discuss the central idea of this work, that is, the manipulation of the skin mode and the realization of SHE. Our experimental scheme is based on an open-boundary Floquet system with the number of unit cells $N=50$ donated as $\mathcal{H}^\text{OBC}(z)$. It should be noticed that under open boundary conditions, the interactions may be slightly modified due to the boundary effect, thus we can not direct employ Eq. \ref{eq-Hinfinity} with a finite truncation to describe the effective Hamiltonian under open boundary conditions. Obviously, the interactions at the two ends should be dramatically modified, which may also be understood in terms of Magnus expansion \cite{blanes2009magnus,goldman2014periodically,jotzu2014experimental,eckardt2015high}. 
Therefore, $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$ is described with modified boudary term (shown in Fig. \ref{fig2}(c)). The boundary term of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$ is given by
%For the open boundary in Fig. \ref{fig2}(c), by numerical simulation, we find that the boundary term of $\mathcal{H}^{\text{OBC}}$ should be replaced by 
\begin{equation}
   M^{\prime} (a^\dagger_1 a_1 - b^\dagger_{N} b_{N}) + t_1^{\prime} (b^\dagger_1 a_1 + b^\dagger_{N} b_{N})+ t_{-1}^{\prime} (a^\dagger_1 b_1 +a^\dagger_N b_{N}),
\end{equation}
and the bulk terms approximately remain $t_{\pm j}$ and $\pm M$ \footnote{$M/\kappa=-0.108i $, $M^{\prime}/\kappa=0.046 - 0.123i $, $t_1/\kappa=0.128 + 0.016i $, $t_1^{\prime}/\kappa=0.121` + 0.036i $, $t_{-1}/\kappa=0.104 +0.068i $, $t_{-1}^{\prime}/\kappa=0.092 + 0.056i$}. It does not alter the bulk's skin effect properties.

This result is stimulating at least in two different aspects. Firstly, it means that the infinite system and the finite system, largely speaking, have the similar structure; however, in realistic systems, we have to taken the boundary effect into account in Floquet system. This is true in any physical system. Secondly, it  implies that we can directly change the boundary interaction without influencing the bulk parameters. The second point is especially important for the realization of SHE. To this end, we consider a system with an end potential on the right end of helical waveguide as $\delta V  c_{2N}^\dagger c_{2N}$ and using Eq. \ref{eq-Heffdis}, the new effective Hamiltonian read as
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}\approx \mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}+\delta V_{\text{eff}} b_{N}^\dagger b_{N},
\end{equation}
where $\delta V_{\text{eff}} \sim \delta V$, see the scheme in Fig. \ref{fig2}(d). Since $\delta V$ is a time-independent small quantity, it will not induce substantial changes to other terms. 

The spectrum of $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$ before applying the end potential is shown in Fig. \ref{fig4}(c). We plot the spatial absolute value distributions of all normalized eigenstates $|\psi_i\rangle$ in Fig. \ref{fig4}(e), showing that all wave functions decay to zero at the right end. We calculated SMT rate  $\chi_i$  in Fig. \ref{fig4}(f), where the index $i$ is sorted in ascending order of the energy real part and the state with the largest SMT is $i_s=50$. Then we choose potential  $\delta V = 0.06i \kappa$ in the rightmost helical waveguide. As a result $\delta V_{\text{eff}} \approx  0.06i \kappa$ and the energies of the states in $\mathcal{H}_\text{F}^{\text {OBC}}$ exhibit different responses. The spectrum of $\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_\text{F}^{\text{OBC}}$ is shown in Fig. \ref{fig4}(d). We can see that the state exhibit the largest response is not the state with the largest amplitude at the rightmost lattice site but rather the state with the largest SMT. It becomes an isolated state in the spectrum as the potential increases and we marked this SHS as $|\tilde{\psi}_{i_s}\rangle$.  This is consistent with our SMT prediction. By controlling the end potential  $\delta V$, we can control the real and imaginary parts of the spectrum of the modified skin mode to ensure that it meets the self-healing condition. The magnitude of the relative gain can be easily modulated in experimental setups.

