Assume the input wave function is $|\Psi(0)\rangle=|\tilde{\psi}_{i_s}\rangle$ and $| \Psi(z)\rangle$ is the evolution of reference wave function without passing through the barrier. $| \Psi^\prime(z)\rangle$ is the evolution of wave function that passes through a barrier, which is a loss of $0.9 \kappa$ applied to the first four waveguides from $z=10 ~\mathrm{mm}$ to $z=25 ~\mathrm{mm}$. The SHE can be characterized by 
$\eta(z)=1-\left |\langle\Psi_\text{norm}(z) \mid \Psi_\text{norm}^{\prime}(z) \rangle\right |$, where $|\Psi_\text{norm}(z)\rangle=|\Psi(z)\rangle /\|\Psi(z)\|$ and $|\Psi^\prime_\text{norm} (z)\rangle=|\Psi^\prime(z)\rangle /\|\Psi^\prime(z)\|$ are normalized wave function. As a comparison with the wave function at $z=0$, we define $\zeta(z)=1-\left |\langle\Psi_\text{norm}(0) \mid \Psi_\text{norm}^{\prime}(z) \rangle\right | $, $\zeta(z)$ is fluctuating and takes equal value to $\eta(z)$ at integer periods indicating its declining process at integer periods. When the state is self-healed, $\eta(z)$ tends to 0 despite being scattered by the barrier. We find that for state other than $|\tilde{\psi}_{i_s}\rangle$, they can not self-heal. We have verified that these results are independent of system sizes.  

{\it Discussion}. In this manuscript, we propose a concept SMT, showing that when the wave functions are fully localized at the one end, the potential at the opposite end can still tune the spectrum of the skin model. This is a fundamental feature closely related to the skin mode contradiction in non-Hermitian models. With this method, we then show how to realize the SHE using parameters in photonic waveguide. The SMT has no Hermitian counterpart and its underlying physics is general, presenting promising opportunities for applications in interdisciplinary systems with non-Hermitian interactions. Notably, even in passive systems without gain (e.g., optical waveguide arrays),  judicious design of global dissipation combined with local modulation can suppress the dissipation of SHS to sufficiently low levels, enabling the observation of SHE under minimal transmission loss.

Further, our method can be utilized to selectively explore the interaction between local potential and the skin mode at arbitrary position, which is not necessary to be at the right end. These findings may provide a potential explanation for some scaling effect of critical behaviors \cite{li2020critical,guo2021exact,liu2021exact} of skin effect and provides a new perspective for sensing \cite{wang2025enhanced} mechanisms in non-Hermitian systems. This will open a new avenue to engineer the skin modes and extends the paradigm of wave function steering in non-Hermitian systems \cite{feng2017non,han2019non}, including their energies and the localization positions,  using local potentials. In the present study, the state most susceptible to end potential remains localize at the left end, yet it can be anticipated that this state will evolve into the right end with much stronger perturbation, in which the transition process and its relation to topology requires some further exploration.

