So naively one may set a paradox-free tachyonic communication platform consisting of an emitter particle of mass $-M$ and receiver particle of mass $M$ for a tachyon signal of mass parameter $m$. The emitter in the frame of $B$ emits a tachyonic signal of mass parameter $m > 0$ towards a receiver at $A$, who is moving away from the emitter at the speed of $v$. Now, due to equation \ref{BIT}, in the frame of the receiver the tachyon signal is moving backwards in-time. But for a tachyon signal moving backwards in-time to be detectable\footnote{Which means they satisfy the conservation of momentum.} it should have a mass parameter $m < 0$, see Table \ref{SD}. Therefore, the signal is undetectable and hence, no communications takes place unless the receiver moves towards the emitter\footnote{The same holds even if one considers an emitter of mass $M$ emitting a tachyon signal of mass paramater $m < 0$ towards a receiver of mass $-M$.}.\\

