\subsection{Tachyon kinematics}
Consider a tachyon that moves forwards in-time with varying speed in coordinate time $t$
\begin{align}
    p^{\mu} = \left(\frac{m}{\sqrt{u^2-1}}, \frac{m\vec{u}}{\sqrt{u^2-1}}\right)
\end{align}
The four-force is given by
\begin{align}
    F^{\mu} = \frac{dp^{\mu}}{d\tau} = \frac{dt}{d\tau} \frac{dp^{\mu}}{dt}= \Gamma \frac{dp^{\mu}}{dt}=  m\left(-\frac{\vec{u}\cdot\vec{a}}{(u^2-1)^{2}}, \frac{\vec{a}}{u^2-1}-\frac{\vec{u}\cdot\vec{a}}{(u^2-1)^{2}}\vec{ u}\right)
\end{align}
where $\vec{a} = \frac{d\vec{u}}{dt}$. Here $\vec{a}$ is the acceleration of the tachyon with respect to the coordinate time. Notice that
\begin{align}
    \vec{u}\cdot\vec{F} = -m\frac{\vec{u}\cdot\vec{a}}{(u^2-1)^2}
\end{align}
This means that a tachyon signal of mass parameter $m > 0$ has the bizzare property that if one applies a force to slow down the tachyon it actually speeds it up. This shows that the coordinate speed is actually the correct observable for the tachyon of mass paramater $m > 0$ and not the proper speed\footnote{Usually referred to as the momentum.}. However, forces are well-defined only in terms of proper speed. Usually, the distinction between coordinate speed and proper speed are not relevant as only proper speed is the relevant Lorentz covariant observable in all particle physics experiments, however, when discussing tachyons this becomes relevant. This also implies that tachyons of mass parameter $m < 0$ have a comparatively more intuitive behaviour. However, we will not discuss more about tachyons of mass parameter $m < 0$ as they are sourced by positive mass particles and therefore, they should have been discovered by now in particle physics experiments.
