\section{Generalities}\label{sec:OpenEFT}    

Open EFTs aim at describing a \textit{system} that interacts with an unspecified \textit{environment}. The environment can either affect the system's dynamics in a unitary manner by generating an effective Hamiltonian, or in a non-unitary manner, inducing dissipation and noise. In this work, after briefly reviewing the Schwinger-Keldysh formalism, we construct a bottom-up open EFT for a scalar and massless tensor. These will eventually be interpreted as an inflaton field and the graviton and we will use our theory to make prediction for cosmological observables. Our approach extends our previous construction of the Open EFToI in the decoupling limit \cite{Salcedo:2024smn} by incorporating dynamical gravity.  Our construction can equivalently be thought of as the most general local EFT for gravity propagating in a material which features a single light scalar degree of freedom. A main conceptual hurdle is how to use the Schwinger-Keldysh formalism to describe the dissipative regime of gauge theories and in particular gravity. We addressed this problem in a companion paper \cite{Salcedo:2024nex} where we discuss and open EFT for a massless spin-one field, namely electromagnetism. Here, we use the results of that paper to study the dynamics of gravity during inflation.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


\subsection{Review of Schwinger-Keldysh formalism}\label{subsec:SK}

The Schwinger-Keldysh formalism \cite{Schwinger:1960qe, Keldysh:1964ud} is a formulation of QFT that allows the description of non-equilibrium systems and/or open systems in which energy and information can be exchanged between the system and the environment \cite{Berges:2004yj, Calzetta:2008iqa, kamenev_2011, breuerTheoryOpenQuantum2002, 2016RPPh...79i6001S}. Also known as in-in or closed-time-path formalism, this construction relies on a path integral contour represented in \Fig{fig:contour} that departs from the familiar in-out contour used in particle-physics (see \cite{Donath:2024utn} for a comparison). In the Schwinger-Keldysh case, the path integral is doubled, with one direction going forward in time denoted as the ``$+$" contour, and one direction going backward in time denoted as the ``$-$" contour. This reflects that common situation in which one is interested in the expectation value of observables in the state of the system, as opposed to for example transition amplitudes.
In the perturbative picture, interactions involving field operators $\phi$ can then be inserted either on the $+$ contour or on the $-$ contour, hence the notation $\phi_\pm$. 

\begin{figure}[tbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{figures/Contour.pdf}
\caption{Illustration of the closed-time-path, where time is running from left to right in both contours and the arrow represent path ordering (time ordering in $\ket{\Omega}$ and anti-time-ordering in $\bra{\Omega}$). We consider an initially pure Bunch-Davies vacuum state $\widehat{\rho}_i = \ket{\Omega}\bra{\Omega}$ at initial time $t_i$.}
    \label{fig:contour}
\end{figure} 

The doubling of the path integral contour is crucial for the formalism to describe both out-of-equilibrium and dissipative phenomena. Indeed, by only requiring the specification of the initial state (which can be either pure or mixed), it allows one to access the out-of-equilibrium dynamics \cite{Berges:2012ty, Berges:2025ccd}.\footnote{While we do not investigate this question in this article, it could be an interesting direction for future work.} Moreover, mixing between the two branches of the path integral is crucial in formulating dissipation and noise in a path integral language \cite{2016RPPh...79i6001S}. The dynamical evolution is specified by an open effective functional
\begin{align}\label{eq:Seffdef}
   S_{\mathrm{eff}}[\phi_+,\phi_-] =  S_\phi[\phi_+] - S_\phi[\phi_-] + S_{\mathrm{IF}}[\phi_+,\phi_-],
\end{align}
whose first two terms correspond to the unitary (\ie Hamiltonian) part, a.k.a. the action, while the third term is the so-called Feynman-Vernon influence functional \cite{FEYNMAN1963118}, which cannot be written as a sum of terms that depend on $\phi_+$ and $\phi_-$ separately. This non-unitary (\ie non-Hamiltonian) part encodes energy and information exchanges between the system and its environment, which has no unitary analogue. In the following we will simply work with the full functional $S_{\mathrm{eff}}$.
To originate from a unitary UV theory, \Eq{eq:Seffdef} must satisfy a set of UV-unitarity constraints (also referred to as non-equilibrium constraints, as they represent non-perturbative conditions valid even far from equilibrium) \cite{Crossley:2015evo, Glorioso:2016gsa, Liu:2018kfw}
\begin{align}
    S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_+,\phi_+\right] &= 0\,, \label{eq:norm1} \\
    S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_+,\phi_-\right] &= - 	S^*_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_-,\phi_+\right] \,,  \label{eq:herm1}\\
    \Ima S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_+,\phi_-\right] &\geq 0 \,, \label{eq:pos1}
\end{align}
which restrict the freedom in constructing bottom-up open EFTs \cite{Hongo:2019qhi, Salcedo:2024smn}.

Observables can be extracted from functional derivatives with respect to external currents of a generating functional. This is conveniently expressed in the so-called Keldysh basis
\begin{align}
    \phi_r = \frac{\phi_+ + \phi_-}{2},\qquad \phi_a = \phi_+ - \phi_- \qquad \Leftrightarrow \qquad \phi_+ = \phi_r + \frac{\phi_a}{2}, \qquad \phi_- = \phi_r - \frac{\phi_a}{2} .
\end{align}
The retarded and advanced components $\phi_r$ and $\phi_a$, which respectively correspond to the mean and the difference of the field inserted along each branch of the path integral, turn out to have a simpler physical interpretation \cite{Kamenev:2009jj}. In this basis, the propagators are the \textit{retarded and advanced Green's function} $G^{R/A}$, which encode how information propagates in the system, and the \textit{Keldysh propagator} $G^K$, which is sensitive to the state of the system. Using these variables, the constraints given in \Eqs{eq:norm1}, \eqref{eq:herm1} and \eqref{eq:pos1} become 
\begin{align}
S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_r,\phi_a = 0\right] &= 0 \,,\label{eq:norm} \\
S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_r,\phi_a\right] &= - 	S^*_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_r,-\phi_a\right] \,,\label{eq:herm} \\
\Ima S_{\mathrm{eff}} \left[\phi_r,\phi_a\right] &\geq 0. \label{eq:pos}
\end{align} 
These constraints also ensure the convergence of the path integral specifying the generating functional 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{Z}[J_{r},J_{a}]=\int_{\Omega}^\pi \mathcal{D}\pir  \int_{\Omega}^{0}\mathcal{D}\pia \ee^{iS_{\mathrm{eff}}[\phi_r, \phi_a] + \int d^{4}x \sqrt{-g}\left(J_{r}\phia+J_{a}\phir\right)},
\end{align}
where $\Omega$ represents the choice of initial state and can be in principle a mixed density matrix \cite{2016RPPh...79i6001S}. The boundary conditions in the path integral are crucial role in ensuring the right number of propagating degrees of freedom, as discussed below. 

Let us now illustrate the scope of the formalism with a simple example based on \cite{Salcedo:2024smn}. We consider a single scalar degree of freedom $\phi$ evolving in flat space according to 
\begin{align}\label{pireq}
    S_{\mathrm{eff}}[\phir,\phia] = \int \dd^4x \left(\dphir \dphia - c_s^2 \partial_i \phir \partial^i \phia + \gamma \dphir \phia + i \beta \phia^2\right),
\end{align}
where a dot represents a time derivatives. Note that both $\gamma$ and $\beta$ should be real, in accordance with the conditions above. Note that the first two terms can be written in a factorized form in the original $+/-$ basis,
\begin{align}
    S_\phi[\phi_+] - S_\phi[\phi_-] \qquad \mathrm{with} \qquad S_\phi[\phi] = \frac{1}{2} \int \dd^4x \left[\dot{\phi}^2 - c_s^2 (\partial_i \phi)^2 \right],
\end{align}
Conversely, the last two terms have no analogue in the unitary case and encode the dissipative and diffusive effects of $S_{\mathrm{IF}}[\phi_+,\phi_-]$ characteristic of the open dynamics. To make this manifest, we derive the equation of motion by varying the effective functional with respect to $\phi_a$.\footnote{Because of \eqref{pireq}, the variation with respect to $\phir$ vanishes on the classical solution $\phia=0$.}  

The only subtlety comes from the noise term $i\beta \phia^2$, which illustrates an important fact about the nature of the advanced field $\phia$. The field $\phi_a$ is \textit{not} a propagating degree of freedom. Indeed, the doubling of the fields in the Schwinger-Keldysh contour is only a technical artifice, the number of dynamical degrees of freedom is unchanged. The field $\phi_a$ is best thought of as an auxiliary field whose role is to characterize the fluctuations of the surrounding environment. This is guaranteed by the path integral boundary conditions that constrain $\phi_a =0$ \textit{both} at the initial and final time.

Then, what is the purpose in life of the even power of $\phi_a$? 
The Hubbard-Stratonovich transformation \cite{Stratonovich1957,Hubbard:1959ub} provides an answer. Upon introducing an auxiliary noise field $\xi$, the open functional can be made to be linear in $\phi_{a}$. The key trick is the mathematical identity
\begin{equation}\label{eq:HStrick}
    \exp\left(-\int d^{4}x \beta\phia^{2}\right)=\mathcal{N}_{0}\int\mathcal{D}\xi\;\exp\left[\int d^{4}x \left(-\frac{\xi^{2}}{4\beta}+i\xi\phia\right)\right],
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{N}_{0}$ being the normalisation constant. It is only at this point that the path integral in $  \phi_{a} $ is well approximated by a stationary phase and that $  \delta S_{\mathrm{eff}}/ \delta \phi_{a} $ can be interpreted as an equation of motion, namely the Langevin equation
\begin{align}\label{eq:langevin}
    \ddot{\phi}_r + \gamma \dphir + c_s^2 k^2 \phi_r = \xi.
\end{align}
The new variable $\xi$ behaves as a Gaussian field with a non-vanishing two-point function
\begin{equation}\label{eq:noisecontact}
    \langle\xi(x)\xi(y)\rangle=2\beta \delta (x-y).
\end{equation}
While \Eq{eq:langevin} is a stochastic differential equation, which may need to be complemented by an interpretation rule (see \eg the \textit{It{\^o}} vs. \textit{Stratonovich} historical controversy \cite{1981JSP....24..175V}), its formulation at the level of the path integral in the Schwinger-Keldysh contour precludes this issue. Moreover, as we will see below, the path integral formulation provides a clear cut prescription for gauge fixing in stochastic systems, which turns out to be crucial for the extension of the formalism to dynamical gravity. 

