\section{World Model and Agent Decision-Making}

World model arises in the context of agent decision-making. An agent is an autonomous system that acts in an environment -- the {\it universe}, with both physical and social worlds -- to achieve a goal (e.g., climbing a mountain, winning a military campaign). We consider an environment with discrete timesteps indexed by $t$ (continuous timesteps can be approximated by infinitesimally small discrete time-steps). Formally, the agent takes the current world state $s_t$ and outputs the next action $a_t$ based on a distribution $p_\pi(a_t | s_t)$, known as the ``policy" in reinforcement learning literature. The optimal agent is thus one that best achieves its goals across all environments. The concept of a World Model arises as a surrogate of the environment in general agentic reasoning. 
