However, MPC can suffer from practical limitations.
% In comparison, it becomes clear that—despite its potential for high-quality decision-making, MPC suffers from practical limitations. 
% Specifically, the simulation of latent trajectories using the world model must be performed repeatedly at every timestep during inference, leading to high computational overhead and making it difficult to respond effectively in fast-changing environments. 
Simulation of latent trajectories using the world model, for instance, must be performed repeatedly at every timestep during inference, leading to high computational overhead and making it difficult to respond effectively in fast-changing environments. 
Beyond computational efficiency, MPC typically plans only a few steps ahead (e.g., up to 10-20 steps) in terms of searching horizon. This limits the extent of its foresight, as long planning horizons (e.g., 100s of steps) can be difficult due to the exploding number of trajectories and world-model errors. 
% \jinyu{Added a bit about difficulty of implementation here.} 
As the horizon increases, MPC also becomes more difficult to implement and optimize, since the proposal distribution must sample entire action sequences at once over the full planning horizon. This is why MPC often relies on relatively simple proposal distributions, such as uniform random sampling or multivariate Gaussians. Indeed, MPC so far has shown promise primarily in simplified settings (e.g., Go) where environment dynamics are simple and slower decision-making is rewarded, but struggles to extend to real-world tasks (e.g., customer service), which typically involve complex dynamics and require a mixture of short- and long-term decision-making. 
% In contrast, model-based RL with world model shifts the computational cost to the training phase. Instead of planning from scratch at each step, the world model is used offline to train a policy network that can later be reused for fast action selection at every state. 
