\subsection{Design Principles}

The PAN world model is built on the following foundational insights:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{All modalities matter}: Text is not optional -- it provides compressed, abstract, and symbolic knowledge that sensory data alone cannot offer. Conversely, raw sensory data such as video or proprioception carries nuances and dynamics absent from text alone. As such, a true model of the world must learn from \textit{all modalities of experience}.
    \item \textbf{Mixed representations enable robust reasoning}: Fixed-sized, continuous embeddings offer fine-grained details, but are often brittle and less scalable. Flexible-length, discrete tokens provide a platform for stable, structured reasoning grounded in long-term memory. PAN thus adopts a hierarchical, mixed representation strategy that dynamically combines both.
    \item \textbf{Autoregressive, generative modeling is essential}: Rather than chasing exact prediction which is unattainable even in the real world, PAN embraces autoregression as a natural model of data generation, and adopts hierarchical abstraction to enable simulation at multiple levels of detail, from sensory perception to strategic reasoning.
    \item \textbf{Latent losses are not enough}: As energy-based latent objectives emerge as prone to collapse and difficult to fix, PAN adopts probabilistic, data reconstruction objectives grounded in observable reality, which provides richer and more stable learning signals.
    \item \textbf{Simulation powers decision-making and learning}: PAN supports flexible integration with model-predictive control as well as reinforcement learning, with the world model serving both as a simulation engine and a training playground for agent behavior. 
\end{itemize}
Below, we describe the architecture and objectives of the PAN world model, and further present our current empirical results in visual simulation and decision-making.

