\subsection{Architecture}

The PAN world model's architecture is a multi-level, modular system composed of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{A tokenizer and encoder} works with an expandable vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$ to take sensory observations $o_t$ (e.g., video, sound, temperature, touch) and estimate the latent world state $\hat{s}_t$ (e.g., objects, tools, agents, emotions). In particular, $\hat{s}_t$ is based on a hierarchical representation $\{\hat{s}_{ti}\}_{i=1}^{N_t}$ consisting of a flexible number of discrete tokens and continuous embeddings, dynamically sized to reflect uncertainty and representational complexity. For instance, a VQ-VAE may map videos to visual tokens, while an LLM-based encoder may use text to represent more abstract concepts.
    \item \textbf{An enhanced LLM backbone} takes action inputs $(a_t, a_{t+1}, ...)$ (e.g., motion, interaction, logistics, or strategy) and implicitly simulates the next states $(\hat{s}_{t+1}, \hat{s}_{t+2}, ...)$ autoregressively. 
    \item When needed, \textbf{a diffusion-based sensory decoder} translates the latent world states back to concrete sensory observations $(\hat{o}_{t+1}, \hat{o}_{t+2}, ...)$, which facilitates inspection, visualization, and alignment with human feedback or other downstream use.
\end{itemize}
This encoder-backbone-decoder architecture supports rich usage for simulation, planning, and interaction, while being general enough to scale across domains.

