The PAN world model is trained with the straightforward but effective generative losses: Data reconstruction loss at each time step aligns the latent spaces and learns the world dynamics jointly between the tokenizer, the world model backbone, and the sensory decoder. As described in Figure (to come), the system reconstructs the observations $(\hat{o}_{t}, \hat{o}_{t+1}, \hat{o}_{t+2}, ...)$ from the simulated world states $(\hat{s}_{t+1}, \hat{s}_{t+2}, ...)$, and compares it to the ground truths $(o_t, o_{t+1}, o_{t+2}, ...)$. This results in the loss function: 
$$\mathcal{L}_{\text{gen}} = \lVert o_t - \hat{o}_t \rVert + \lVert o_{t+1} - \hat{o}_{t+1} \rVert + ...$$
As proven in Sec.\ref{subsec:objective}, generative losses grounded in observation evidence are resistant to model collapse and provides powerful learning signals for general applicability.

