PAN is not only a simulator, but also a foundation for agent reasoning and learning. In our recent implementation (cite), we showcase how PAN-style world models power real-world capabilities such as web browsing. As shown in Figure M:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{An LLM-based tokenizer} translates webpage screenshots and HTML into natural language descriptions (e.g., \texttt{Google Flights interface with \underline{empty} Destination field}).
    \item Given an action described in text (e.g., \texttt{Fill "Los Angeles" into Destination}), \textbf{an LLM-based world model} generates the next state of the browser described in natural language (e.g., \texttt{Google Flights interface with \underline{"Los Angeles"} in Destination field}).
    \item \textbf{No decoder} is used for planning as the text description contains the necessary information for decision-making.
\end{itemize}
Based on the PAN-style world model, the resulting ReasonerAgent-Web (cite) can perform complex tasks like searching for flights for travel planning, compiling online shopping options, and researching news coverage, all based on internal simulation rather than roll-out in the true environment. This is an example of the PAN-Agent architecture with world model as a component, which we will discuss in detail in another paper. 

Going forward, the PAN-style world model may serve not just as a reasoning engine, but also a training environment, where one can deploy agents and shape their policies using RL and other learning algorithms. The domain is not just limited to web browsing, but also physical robotics, strategic planning, and beyond. Agents trained through experience generated by PAN can also flexibly mix rapid reaction and deliberate planning, moving one step closer to human-level autonomy.

\subsection{Comparison and Outlook}
In contrast to the critiqued framework which remains confined to narrow, toyish environments and tasks (e.g., toy mazes (cite), robotic arms (cite), and physical violation detection; see Figure K), the PAN world model scales to complex, multimodal, and real-world tasks. Its simulation quality, abstraction handling, and integration with downstream agents make it a promising foundation for general intelligence grounded in experience and imagination. 

Future work will further expand PAN's integration with learning agents and real-world grounding via human interaction. On the model side, better integration of multi-level representations and support for hierarchical planning is still a challenging question. Overall, we believe that this trajectory of simulation-based reasoning and learning is a necessary step towards robust, scalable, and general-purpose AI agents.

